## Core Principles
- Use utility-first approach for styling instead of custom CSS
- Embrace the utility-first workflow before reaching for custom abstractions
- Use the mobile-first responsive design approach
- Maintain consistency by using Tailwind's design system
- Follow the "just enough design" principle with minimal custom CSS
- Learn the utility class naming conventions to increase development speed

## Components & Reusability
- Create custom components using @apply directive for repeated UI patterns
- Extract components to avoid duplicating the same utility patterns
- Use Blade components to encapsulate both markup and styling
- Create a consistent design language with reusable components
- Balance component abstraction with utility flexibility
- Use slot elements for component customization

## Responsive Design
- Utilize Tailwind's responsive utilities (sm:, md:, lg:, xl:)
- Design for mobile first, then add breakpoint-specific utilities
- Test on multiple screen sizes during development
- Use Tailwind's container class appropriately for layout containers
- Implement responsive typography with appropriate size scales
- Keep layouts fluid rather than fixed where possible

## Organization
- Keep CSS files organized and modular
- Group related utility classes together for readability
- Sort utility classes in a consistent order (layout, typography, visual, etc.)
- Use comments to document complex utility combinations
- Separate custom component styles into dedicated files
- Use meaningful class prefixes for custom utilities

## Theming & Customization
- Extend the Tailwind theme in tailwind.config.js instead of using custom CSS
- Define custom colors and spacing scales in configuration
- Use CSS variables for dynamic theming capabilities
- Create consistent color palettes using Tailwind's color system
- Use semantic color naming in your theme configuration

## Performance
- Optimize Tailwind configuration for production with PurgeCSS
- Configure content paths properly to avoid missing styles
- Minimize custom CSS to benefit from Tailwind's tree-shaking
- Consider using JIT mode for development and production
- Monitor final CSS bundle size and optimize where necessary
- Use modern CSS features that Tailwind supports (like flex and grid)
